while people in the Panhandle are  returning home to see what if anything  is left some communities were flattened  while others were mostly spared it will  take weeks to clean up and assess the  damage and months or even years to  rebuild Nicky batiste  is in Tallahassee after a first-hand  look at the devastation Nicky good  morning good morning here in Tallahassee  we are seeing some damage like this  trees down and power out yesterday we  were an hour away in Bristol where the  damage is even more severe we went with  people heading home for the first time  since the hurricane hit after hunkering  down in safe places and they were  nervous to see what they would find  well my clothes are in there and the  dresses but they're all messed up  peeking into her bedroom for their first  time since hurricane Michael destroyed  it four-year-old Armani is upset her  pink toys are now covered in debris when  you look in there how do you feel inside  Wow her mother Brittney chamber says  when she first saw the damage to her  home in Bristol she broke down crying  [ __ ] overwhelming come home and find her  home like this as the storm hit she took  her daughter with her to her job as a  caretaker at a nursing home nearby when  she came home her entire street was  littered with downed trees and power  lines that couldn't drive home I had to  literally park my car and walk all the  way here it's just like my heart was  sinking in my stomach  we met chambers after we visited her  neighbor Perla ol Vera it was just  debris everywhere  her house is still standing yes and that  was my relief my house is still standing  we first met Oh Vera and her twin  daughters at a local shelter we all  shared during the hurricane she says she  prayed there that her home and family  would be safe do you think your prayers  were answered yes and like I said up  there like the Lord put his hands on  like Oh Vera Chambers is grateful  despite the damage to her home  materialistic things that can 